{"step_index":197,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"CHECKPOINT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T19:31:12Z","content":"{{ CHECKPOINT 2 }}\n **The earlier parts of this conversation have been truncated due to its long length. The following content summarizes the truncated context so that you may continue your work. **\n\n\n# User Requests\nThe following were user requests from the truncated conversation in chronological order:\n1. Copy and paste this exact prompt into Antigravity to combine both features into a unified, high-tech command center using a tabbed interface:\n\n\"I want to keep our existing 'Live Conjunction Analysis' table but add the new 'Mission Risk Calculator' alongside it. Let's build a unified, single-page command center with a sleek Tabbed interface.\n\nPlease update index.html, app.js, and the Tailwind CSS with these exact requirements:\n\nTabbed Navigation: Below the top minimalist header, create a sleek, glassmorphism tab menu with two options: 'LIVE TRACKING' and 'RISK CALCULATOR'. Ensure the active tab has a glowing bottom border to indicate which view is open.\n\nView 1: Live Tracking (Existing Feature): Move our current data table (the one fetching from FastAPI with the Red/Yellow/Green dynamic badges and the simulated 1.2km critical risk) into this first tab. It should look exactly like our premium glass panel design.\n\nView 2: Risk Calculator (New Feature): When the second tab is clicked, hide the table and show t\n<truncated 897 bytes>\n2. \"We need to upgrade the FastAPI backend to use real aerospace physics instead of mock data, so the judges can see genuine mathematical risk modeling.Please update main.py and sgp4_calc.py to include the following:Live Tracking Math (Conjunction Probability): For the existing SGP4 tracking endpoint, calculate the Probability of Collision ($P_c$) for each close approach. Assume a combined object radius of 5 meters and use a simplified 2D Gaussian probability integral based on the miss distance and a simulated positional covariance (e.g., 500 meters of uncertainty).New Endpoint for the Risk Calculator: Create a new POST endpoint at /api/mission-risk that accepts JSON input: altitude_km, area_m2, and time_years.Poisson Mission Risk Formula: In the /api/mission-risk endpoint, calculate the probability of at least one debris impact using the Poisson distribution.First, determine the debris flux ($F$) based on the altitude input (e.g., $F = 5.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ for 400 km, scaling up to $3.5 \\times 10^{-4}$ for 80\n<truncated 358 bytes>\n3. \"Let's upgrade the backend with real aerospace physics and add our new risk calculation API.Please update sgp4_calc.py and main.py with the following:Probability of Collision ($P_c$) in Conjunction Tracking:In sgp4_calc.py, add a function to calculate the 2D Gaussian encounter plane collision probability ($P_c$).Assume combined collision radius $R = 10\\text{ m}$ ($0.01\\text{ km}$) and combined positional uncertainty (standard deviation) $\\sigma = 0.5\\text{ km}$ ($500\\text{ m}$).Calculate $P_c \\approx 1 - \\exp\\left(-\\frac{R^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{d^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right)\\right)$ or standard encounter plane Gaussian approximation for miss distance $d$.Include this calculated collision_probability (formatted as scientific notation, e.g., 1.42e-4) in the conjunction results for each pair.Mission Risk Calculator Endpoint:In main.py, create a Pydantic model MissionRiskRequest with fields:altitude_km: floatarea_m2: floattime_years: floatCreate a POST /api/mission-risk endpoint that computes:Debris flux $F$ (\n<truncated 937 bytes>\n4. \"Add a new tab called '3D ORBIT VIEWER'. Integrate the open-source CesiumJS library. Pass the live TLE data from our FastAPI backend to the frontend, and use Cesium to plot a 3D Earth with the active satellites represented as glowing dots. When a user clicks a row in the 'Live Tracking' table, make the 3D globe automatically pan and zoom to that specific satellite's current coordinates.\"\n5. The CesiumJS globe is not rendering correctly when I switch to the '3D ORBIT VIEWER' tab. This is a known WebGL issue where the canvas breaks if initialized while its parent container has display: none.\n\nPlease update the app.js tab-switching logic to fix this:\n\nWhen the '3D ORBIT VIEWER' tab is clicked, make the container visible in the DOM before rendering the globe.\n\nIf the viewer is already initialized, trigger viewer.resize() immediately after the tab becomes visible so it recalculates its dimensions.\n\nTo prevent background crashes, pause the render loop when the tab is hidden (viewer.useDefaultRenderLoop = false) and resume it when the tab is shown (viewer.useDefaultRenderLoop = true).\n\nAction: Update the JavaScript, click the tab in your embedded browser to verify the globe renders correctly, and take a screenshot.\"\n6. \"The 3D globe is rendering as a solid blue sphere instead of a realistic Earth. This is likely an imagery provider issue. We need to make the globe look realistic and highly interactive.\n\nPlease update the CesiumJS initialization in app.js with the following:\n\nRealistic Earth (No Token Needed): Configure the Cesium Viewer to use a free imagery provider that doesn't require an Ion token. Use Cesium.ArcGisMapServerImageryProvider pointing to the Esri World Imagery service to get a photorealistic Earth texture.\n\nOrbital Trails: Instead of just showing a static dot for the satellites, draw their orbital paths using Cesium.PolylineGraphics to show their trajectory over the next 90 minutes.\n\nInteractive Info Panels: Make the satellite entities clickable. When a user clicks an object, the Cesium InfoBox should pop up displaying its specific data (Name, Type, and Risk Status).\n\nCamera Tracking: Ensure that double-clicking an object locks the camera to it, allowing the user to seamlessly follow the satellite as the\n<truncated 234 bytes>\n7. \"The 3D globe is still rendering as a solid blue sphere. The ArcGisMapServerImageryProvider is failing because newer Cesium versions require an ArcGIS access token by default.\n\nPlease update app.js to use a direct, token-free tile URL for the imagery.\n\nRemove the current ArcGIS imagery provider.\n\nIn the Cesium.Viewer constructor options, set the baseLayer (or imageryProvider depending on syntax) to use a Cesium.UrlTemplateImageryProvider instead.\n\nPoint it to this free, public Esri tile server URL:\nurl: '[https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer/tile/](https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer/tile/){z}/{y}/{x}'\n\nEnsure maximumLevel: 19 is set in the provider options.\n\nAction: Update the JavaScript. Once the server reloads, click the '3D ORBIT VIEWER' tab, wait a moment for the photorealistic Earth texture to load, and take a new screenshot.\n8. \"The realistic Earth texture is great, but the scene feels static and flat. We need to add a starry sky, atmospheric glow, and a slow auto-rotation to make it cinematic.\n\nPlease update the app.js Cesium initialization with these exact features:\n\nAtmosphere & Day/Night Cycle: Enable real-time sun lighting by setting viewer.scene.globe.enableLighting = true. This creates a realistic day/night terminator line across the globe.\n\nGlowing Halo: Ensure viewer.scene.skyAtmosphere is active so we get that thin blue atmospheric glow on the Earth's edge. The default Cesium SkyBox will handle the stars.\n\nAuto-Rotate (Idle Animation): Hook into the clock tick to make the globe slowly spin on its own. Add an event listener to viewer.clock.onTick and call viewer.scene.camera.rotateRight(0.0005) on every frame.\n\nAction: Write the updated JavaScript. Wait for the server to refresh, confirm the globe is spinning with proper lighting, and take a new screenshot.\"\n9. \"The globe is still blue. Cesium is failing to load the default imagery because it is asking for an Ion token. We need to forcefully bypass this and inject a 4K photorealistic satellite texture, along with cinematic lighting.\n\nPlease completely rewrite the Cesium.Viewer initialization in app.js with the following configuration:\n\nDisable Default Providers: When initializing the viewer, set baseLayerPicker: false, imageryProvider: false, and geocoder: false to stop it from requesting token-gated assets.\n\nInject 4K Imagery: After creating the viewer, manually add a high-resolution, token-free Esri satellite layer using this exact code:\n\nJavaScript\nviewer.imageryLayers.addImageryProvider(new Cesium.UrlTemplateImageryProvider({\n    url : 'https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer/tile/{z}/{y}/{x}',\n    maximumLevel : 19\n}));\nCinematic Atmosphere & Stars: Enable the sun and atmosphere to make it look realistic.\n\nviewer.scene.globe.enableLighting = true; (creates realistic day/n\n<truncated 441 bytes>\n10. \"We are adding a 'Collision Avoidance Maneuver (CAM) Planner' to our Live Tracking tab. In real operations, avoidance maneuvers are very small thrusts, typically applied along the track of the orbit, to maximize distance from a potential conjunction. Satellites frequently use these delta-v burns for station-keeping and collision avoidance.\n\nPlease update index.html, app.js, and the Tailwind CSS with these exact features:\n\nCAM Drawer UI: Add a hidden right-side slide-out drawer (using a glassmorphism style: bg-black/80, backdrop-blur-lg, right-aligned). Add a close button.\n\nTrigger Mechanism: Update the 'Live Tracking' table so that clicking any row with a 'CRITICAL' risk badge opens the CAM Drawer and populates it with the selected objects' names.\n\nThrust Controls (Delta-v): Inside the drawer, build an interactive slider labeled 'In-Track Delta-v (m/s)' ranging from 0.0 to 2.0 m/s with a step of 0.1. Display the current slider value in a glowing text span next to the label.\n\nDynamic Simulation Chart: Belo\n<truncated 767 bytes>\n\n# Previous Session Summary:\n### 1. Outstanding User Requests\n\n- **Implement CAM (Collision Avoidance Maneuver) Planner Logic:** \"Write vanilla JavaScript to simulate the maneuver: as the user drags the slider, multiply the Delta-v value by a mock expansion factor (e.g., 50 km per 0.1 m/s) and add it to the original miss distance. Update the visual bar and a 'New Miss Distance (km)' text metric in real-time. If the newly calculated miss distance exceeds 50 km, change the simulated risk badge inside the drawer from 'CRITICAL' (Red) to 'SAFE' (Green).\"\n  - **Status:** IMPLEMENTATION (HTML markup is done; JS logic in `app.js` is pending).\n- **CAM Trigger Mechanism:** \"Update the 'Live Tracking' table so that clicking any row with a 'CRITICAL' risk badge opens the CAM Drawer and populates it with the selected objects' names.\"\n  - **Status:** IMPLEMENTATION.\n- **Take a screenshot of the CAM UI:** \"Once running, use your embedded browser to click a CRITICAL row, drag the Delta-v slider to 1.5 m/s, and take a screenshot.\"\n  - **Status:** NOT STARTED / SUGGESTED (Reminder: As an AI, inform the user to refresh their browser instead of taking a screenshot).\n\n### 2. User Knowledge\n\n- **Goal:** Space Debris and Collision Avoidance web prototype for a hackathon. Acting as Lead Full-Stack Developer.\n- **Tech Stack:** Python (FastAPI), Vanilla HTML/JS, Tailwind CSS, CesiumJS, `satellite.js`.\n- **Physics Models (Strict Requirements):** \n  - Conjunction Probability of Collision ($P_c$) formula: $P_c \\approx 1 - \\exp\\left(-\\frac{R^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{d^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right)\\right)$ with $R=10m (0.01km), \\sigma=0.5km$.\n  - Mission Risk uses a Poisson distribution where Flux ($F$) is $5.0\\times 10^{-5}$ (<500km), $3.5\\times 10^{-4}$ (500-900km), and $1.0\\times 10^{-4}$ (>900km).\n- **Cesium Configuration Rules:**\n  - **MUST** disable default providers to prevent Ion token errors: `imageryProvider: false, baseLayerPicker: false, geocoder: false`.\n  - **MUST** inject 4K imagery manually: `viewer.imageryLayers.addImageryProvider(new Cesium.UrlTemplateImageryProvider({url: 'https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer/tile/{z}/{y}/{x}', maximumLevel: 19}))`.\n  - **MUST** pause render loop when the 3D tab is hidden (`viewer.useDefaultRenderLoop = false`) and trigger `viewer.resize()` when unhidden.\n- **Styling:** SpaceX-style, cinematic, dark mode, glassmorphism (`bg-black/40`, `backdrop-blur-md`). Inter font.\n\n### 3. Work Accomplished\n\n- **Fixed API Bugs:** Corrected the `sgp4()` argument mismatch bug that was causing 500 errors.\n- **Physics Backend:** Updated `sgp4_calc.py` with real $P_c$ math (returned in scientific notation) and injected a mock 1.2km collision for `STARLINK-1007` vs `COSMOS 2251 DEB`. Added `POST /api/mission-risk` to `main.py` for Poisson math.\n- **UI Overhaul:** Rebuilt the entire frontend into a tabbed \"Command Center\" featuring \"Live Tracking\", a \"Risk Calculator\", and a \"3D Orbit Viewer\".\n- **3D Orbit Viewer:** Successfully integrated CesiumJS with `satellite.js`. Fetches TLEs from `GET /api/tles`, plots 90-minute orbital trails (`PolylineGraphics`), and includes atmospheric lighting, star skybox, and idle auto-rotation.\n- **CAM Drawer UI:** Added the HTML markup and Tailwind CSS for the right-side slide-out CAM drawer to `frontend/index.html`.\n\n### 4. Model Knowledge\n\n- **Uvicorn Reloading Quirks:** `uvicorn --reload` inside this sandbox virtual environment sometimes fails to pick up code changes. It is safer to `kill` the background task (using `manage_task`) and restart the server manually with `run_command` when making backend changes.\n- **Cesium Camera Tracking:** Double-clicking an entity in Cesium naturally tracks it if `infoBox` and `selectionIndicator` are true. The `viewer.scene.camera.rotateRight(0.0005)` in the `onTick` event creates a beautiful cinematic orbit around tracked entities.\n- **Tab Switching Logic:** `switchTab()` in `app.js` manages both DOM visibility and Cesium WebGL lifecycle states (`resize()`, `useDefaultRenderLoop`). \n\n### 5. Files and Code\n\n- **Edited Files:**\n  - `/Users/anandgopaltiwary/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/space_debris_prototype/backend/main.py`: Added `GET /api/tles` and `POST /api/mission-risk`.\n  - `/Users/anandgopaltiwary/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/space_debris_prototype/backend/sgp4_calc.py`: Implemented 2D Gaussian $P_c$ integral and mock risk triggers.\n  - `/Users/anandgopaltiwary/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/space_debris_prototype/frontend/index.html`: Completely rewritten. Contains the Tab UI, CDN links (Cesium 1.110, satellite.js 5.0.0, Tailwind), and the recently added (hidden) CAM Drawer markup (`#camDrawer`).\n  - `/Users/anandgopaltiwary/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/space_debris_prototype/frontend/app.js`: Contains tab switching, FastAPI fetch logic, Cesium instantiation (`initCesium`), and table rendering. **Needs updates for CAM.**\n\n- **Viewed Files:**\n  - `/Users/anandgopaltiwary/.gemini/config/plugins/modern-web-guidance-plugin/skills/modern-web-guidance/SKILL.md`: Consulted to ensure UI implementations followed modern web standards (utilized standard Tailwind classes for glassmorphism and keyframe animations).\n  - `/Users/anandgopaltiwary/.gemini/antigravity/brain/7be09992-af56-4486-bcae-bcfc2545af88/implementation_plan.md`: Updated to seek user approval before integrating CesiumJS.\n\n### 6. Current Work and Next Steps\n\n- **Current Task:** I was in the middle of fulfilling the user's request to add the Collision Avoidance Maneuver (CAM) logic. The HTML is written, but the JavaScript is missing.\n- **Next Step:** Edit `/Users/anandgopaltiwary/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/space_debris_prototype/frontend/app.js`.\n  1. Add DOM selectors for the new CAM elements (`#camDrawer`, `#closeCamDrawer`, `#camObject1`, `#camObject2`, `#camDeltaVSlider`, `#camDeltaVVal`, `#camNewDistVal`, `#camDistBar`, `#camRiskBadge`).\n  2. Inside `renderTable()`, update the row 